-- emit/release/app: a release build shares a validator only from the
-- number of calls at which it makes the program smaller (`schema.md` §6,
-- *Validators shared across schemas*; `SchemaShare`).
--
-- `User` reads four required strings, so the build shares `badString`: each
-- string field keeps its read and its tests in place, as one condition, and
-- calls the helper only when one fails. Once a field helper is shared the
-- depth bound's and the missing key's helpers are paid for, and the
-- integer field's missing key calls the latter. One integer is under the
-- four calls `badInt` needs, so its type tests and their issues stay
-- written in place. The root is read at depth 0, so specialisation folds
-- every depth test of this worker away, the helper's included.
import Node exposing (Program)


schema User =
    name : String as "user-name"
    email : String
    bio : String
    city : String
    age : Int


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines
        [ case
            User.parse
                "{\"user-name\":\"a\",\"email\":\"b\",\"bio\":\"c\",\"city\":\"d\",\"age\":1}"
          of
            Ok u →
                u.name ++ String.fromInt u.age

            Err _ →
                "failed"
        ]
